Let $v(x)=x^{^{{\scriptsize\dfrac14}}}$, then $[u(x)]^{^{{\scriptsize\dfrac14}}}=v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)$, or $(v\circ u)(x)$.

Therefore, the derivative of $[u(x)]^{^{{\scriptsize\dfrac14}}}$ can be found using **the chain rule**:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)]^{^{{\scriptsize\dfrac14}}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]&&\gray{\text{Let }v(x)=x^{^{{\scriptsize\dfrac14}}}}
\\\\
&=v'\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\text{The chain rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{4}[u(x)]^{^{-\scriptsize\dfrac{3}{4}}}\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(x^{^{{\scriptsize\dfrac14}}}\right)=\dfrac{1}{4}x^{^{-\scriptsize\dfrac{3}{4}}}}
\end{align}$
